' SIMULATIONS
' Three simulations from the "Simulations" chapter of Tim Hartnell's Giant
' Book of Computer Games (Ballantine Books, 1984; Interface Publications,
' 1983), for the ColecoVision in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' MISTRESS OF XENOPHOBIA (after a program by Alastair Gourlay): rule a
' little planet for 20 years. Each year decree how much more land to farm
' and how much to spend on food ($10 a unit each); feed the peasants or
' face rebellion; a population under 150, or an empty treasury, ends it.
' RURAL PURSUITS (after a program by Stephen Glen): run a farm for 10
' years. Choose the acres to work, the workers' pay and the share of corn,
' wheat and barley; badly paid workers leave.
' CHAIRMAN OF THE BOARD: run a factory week by week (hire, fire, set
' production, raise prices) until stock and capital pass $10,000, while
' unions, fires and suppliers do their worst.
'
' Controls (either joystick): numbers are entered digit by digit: left and
' right choose a digit, up and down change it, the button confirms. The
' second button leaves. Keypad not used.
'
' Liberties taken:
' - All three: whole numbers (and cents in CHAIRMAN) instead of BASIC's
'   fractions; RND is a 16-bit fraction. Sums are capped at 999,999,999.
' - XENOPHOBIA: RND*(W*R*5 - P) is computed on the size of the change,
'   up or down, then added or taken away.
' - RURAL PURSUITS: the pay is whole dollars, up to $255 a worker.
' - CHAIRMAN: the listing's HA (lines 1240-1250) is read as MA, so the
'   kazoos made are counted into stock; a fire cannot burn stock that is
'   not there, and with no stock nothing is sold (the listing would loop).
' - New pictures (the planet, the farm, the factory), bar graphs, music
'   (Affairs of State) and sounds.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22; sprites only for
' the celebration (eight sparks, staggered); every screen is drawn with the
' NMI off after a wait.

